From: Manuel de Brito Fontes <aledbf@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 14:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 9/11] cpu/hotplug: Poll for a booting AP by local_clock()

cpuhp_wait_for_sync_state() polls for an AP for one millisecond by
ktime_get() and then sleeps for one to two. While the system boots, the
clocksource is still jiffies and hrtimers are not in high resolution
mode, so the millisecond of polling is anything up to one tick, and the
sleep ends on a tick boundary one to three ticks later. An AP that takes
a little over a millisecond to report alive, as one does under a
hypervisor where each of its early steps traps, costs the boot CPU two
to three ticks.

Time the polling by local_clock(), which has nanosecond resolution this
early, and while the system boots poll for up to 20 ms before sleeping:
nothing else is waiting for the boot CPU then, and an AP that comes up
at all comes up well within that. Outside of boot the poll is the one
millisecond it was meant to be. On bare metal, where an AP reports alive
well inside a millisecond, nothing changes.

The gap it is for: "CPUs: #1" to "smp: Brought up 1 node, 2 CPUs" was
4.1 ms nested under KVM on the bench fleet against under 1.4 ms on bare
metal, same CPU (lab runs 44563496308, 44563512999). Not measured to
move yet: in a guest on a laptop, not nested, the AP is up in 0.1-0.2
ms and the poll never reaches the sleep. A CPU hot-added after boot
(task boot:hotadd) still comes online.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 kernel/cpu.c | 18 ++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/kernel/cpu.c
+++ b/kernel/cpu.c
@@ -3,6 +3,7 @@
  * (C) 2001, 2002, 2003, 2004 Rusty Russell
  */
 #include <linux/sched/mm.h>
+#include <linux/sched/clock.h>
 #include <linux/proc_fs.h>
 #include <linux/smp.h>
 #include <linux/init.h>
@@ -310,10 +311,23 @@ static bool cpuhp_wait_for_sync_state(un
 {
 	atomic_t *st = per_cpu_ptr(&cpuhp_state.ap_sync_state, cpu);
 	ktime_t now, end, start = ktime_get();
+	u64 poll_start = local_clock();
+	u64 poll_ns = NSEC_PER_MSEC;
 	int sync;
 
 	end = start + 10ULL * NSEC_PER_SEC;
 
+	/*
+	 * While the system boots, ktime_get() runs on the jiffies
+	 * clocksource and sleeps end on a tick, so a millisecond of polling
+	 * is anything up to one tick and the sleep after it one to three.
+	 * Nothing else wants this CPU yet: poll for longer, timed by
+	 * local_clock(). An AP that takes over a millisecond to come up, as
+	 * one does under a hypervisor, no longer costs the boot ticks.
+	 */
+	if (system_state == SYSTEM_BOOTING)
+		poll_ns = 20 * NSEC_PER_MSEC;
+
 	sync = atomic_read(st);
 	while (1) {
 		if (sync == state) {
@@ -326,8 +340,8 @@ static bool cpuhp_wait_for_sync_state(un
 		if (now > end) {
 			/* Timeout. Leave the state unchanged */
 			return false;
-		} else if (now - start < NSEC_PER_MSEC) {
-			/* Poll for one millisecond */
+		} else if (local_clock() - poll_start < poll_ns) {
+			/* Poll for a while before sleeping */
 			arch_cpuhp_sync_state_poll();
 		} else {
 			usleep_range(USEC_PER_MSEC, 2 * USEC_PER_MSEC);
